### What is the Structurally Unique BSTs problem?

Given an integer N, generate all structurally unique BSTs that store the values 1 through N. Each tree must satisfy the BST ordering invariant: for every node, all values in the left subtree are smaller and all values in the right subtree are larger.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class UniqueBSTs {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static List<TreeNode> generateTrees(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return new ArrayList<>();
        return generate(1, n);
    }

    private static List<TreeNode> generate(int start, int end) {
        List<TreeNode> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (start > end) {
            result.add(null); // empty subtree sentinel
            return result;
        }
        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            List<TreeNode> leftTrees = generate(start, i - 1);
            List<TreeNode> rightTrees = generate(i + 1, end);
            for (TreeNode left : leftTrees) {
                for (TreeNode right : rightTrees) {
                    TreeNode root = new TreeNode(i);
                    root.left = left;
                    root.right = right;
                    result.add(root);
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<TreeNode> trees = generateTrees(3);
        System.out.println("Number of unique BSTs for N=3: " + trees.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Number of unique BSTs for N=3: 5
```

### Why does the BST ordering property drive the recursion?

When you choose value i as the root, the BST invariant forces all values less than i into the left subtree and all values greater than i into the right subtree. This splits the range [start, end] into [start, i-1] and [i+1, end], which is a natural divide-and-conquer.

```java
// Choosing root i from [1, 3]:
// i=1: left=[], right=[2,3]
// i=2: left=[1], right=[3]
// i=3: left=[1,2], right=[]

// For i=2:
// Left subtrees of [1]: just {1}
// Right subtrees of [3]: just {3}
// Pairing: one tree with root=2, left=1, right=3
```

### Why is the empty-range sentinel critical?

When start > end, the range is empty, meaning no subtree exists. Returning a list with one null lets the pairing loops execute once even when a subtree is absent. Without this, the pairing loop would produce zero trees when one side is empty, losing valid trees where the root has only one child.

```java
// WRONG: returning empty list for empty range
if (start > end) return new ArrayList<>(); // empty list
// Pairing: for (left in []) for (right in [...]) -> nothing produced!
// We lose all trees where this side has no child

// CORRECT: return list with one null
if (start > end) {
    result.add(null); // sentinel for absent subtree
    return result;
}
// Pairing: for (left in [null]) for (right in [...]) -> correct trees produced
```

### How many unique BSTs are there?

The number of unique BST shapes for N values is the Nth Catalan number: C(N) = (2N)! / ((N+1)! * N!). This grows exponentially, so generation is practical only for small N.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CatalanNumber {
    public static int numTrees(int n) {
        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
        dp[0] = 1; dp[1] = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                dp[i] += dp[j] * dp[i - 1 - j];
            }
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int n = 1; n <= 8; n++) {
            System.out.println("N=" + n + ": " + numTrees(n) + " unique BSTs");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
N=1: 1 unique BSTs
N=2: 2 unique BSTs
N=3: 5 unique BSTs
N=4: 14 unique BSTs
N=5: 42 unique BSTs
N=6: 132 unique BSTs
N=7: 429 unique BSTs
N=8: 1430 unique BSTs
```

### How is counting different from generation?

Counting uses dynamic programming: dp[i] = sum of dp[j] * dp[i-1-j] for j in [0, i-1]. This runs in O(n^2) time and O(n) space. Generation must build actual tree nodes, which takes O(C(n) * n) time and space because each of the C(n) trees has n nodes.

| Aspect | Counting (numTrees) | Generation (generateTrees) |
|---|---|---|
| Result | Single integer | List of tree roots |
| Time | O(n^2) | O(C(n) * n) |
| Space | O(n) | O(C(n) * n) for tree nodes |
| Memoization | Straightforward | Memoizes lists of roots per range |

```java
// Counting: dp table, O(n^2)
// dp[3] = dp[0]*dp[2] + dp[1]*dp[1] + dp[2]*dp[0] = 2+1+2 = 5

// Generation: actual tree construction
// For N=3: build 5 distinct TreeNode trees
```

### What are the edge cases?

N=0 returns an empty list (no trees). N=1 returns a single tree with just the root. The null sentinel handles all one-child cases internally.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UniqueBSTsEdge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("N=0: " + UniqueBSTs.generateTrees(0).size()); // 0
        System.out.println("N=1: " + UniqueBSTs.generateTrees(1).size()); // 1
        System.out.println("N=2: " + UniqueBSTs.generateTrees(2).size()); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
N=0: 0
N=1: 1
N=2: 2
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is returning an empty list instead of a list with null for the empty range. This causes the pairing loop to produce nothing when one side is absent.

```java
// WRONG:
if (start > end) return new ArrayList<>();
// Result: trees with one-child roots are never generated

// CORRECT:
if (start > end) { result.add(null); return result; }
```

### When should you use this technique?

Use this divide-and-conquer approach when you need to enumerate all valid tree structures. In banking, it applies to benchmarking lookup trees for interest-tier configurations.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TierBenchmark {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int tiers = 5;
        var trees = UniqueBSTs.generateTrees(tiers);
        System.out.println("Unique tier-lookup trees for " + tiers + " tiers: " + trees.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Unique tier-lookup trees for 5 tiers: 42
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should explain the Catalan number formula, the difference between counting and generation, and why the empty-range sentinel is essential for correctness. They should also note that generation is exponential and only practical for small N.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:
- How many unique BSTs for N values? (Catalan number.)
- Can you just count without generating? (Yes, DP in O(n^2).)
- Why return [null] for empty range? (Pairing loop needs to execute once.)
- What is the time complexity of generation? (O(C(n) * n), exponential.)
